## VI. CONCLUSION & INSTITUTIONAL ALLOCATION DIRECTIVE

Neuralink Corp. represents an acute structural mispricing of private capital driven by the conflation of exponential software scaling with high-friction Class III neurosurgery:

1. **Statutory Barriers Are Absolute:** FDA Class III risk-benefit statutes (21 CFR 860.7 / 21 CFR 56.111) permanently prohibit elective consumer craniotomies, locking Neuralink out of the consumer AI market ($0 TAM) and confining it to a <$1.5B severe clinical paralysis niche capped by CMS DRG reimbursement bundles.
2. **Surgical Unit Economics Favor Minimally Invasive Competitors:** Penetrating parenchymal threads requiring full craniotomies and proprietary R1 robotic CapEx cannot compete on hospital throughput, procedural cost, or safety against 20-minute jugular catheterizations (Synchron) or FDA 510(k)-cleared reversible surface arrays (Precision Neuroscience).
3. **Chronic Biological & Balance Sheet Tail Risk:** Unresolved parenchymal gliosis (ISO 10993), mandatory 5-to-7-year lithium-ion battery revision surgeries, and commercial product liability exclusions leave equity holders exposed to uncapped balance sheet indemnification risk.

**INSTITUTIONAL DIRECTIVE:** 
* **Private Equity / Venture Allocation Desks:** Abstain from primary or secondary liquidity participation above a $1.25B enterprise valuation cap; mark existing Series D/E book holdings down by **85% to reflect public MedTech peer multiples (~$1.22B Fair Implied Value)**.
* **Sector Reallocation:** Redirect neurotechnology venture capital strictly toward minimally invasive endovascular (Synchron), reversible surface micro-ECoG (Precision Neuroscience), or hermetically sealed high-durability clinical architectures operating with zero proprietary hospital CapEx friction.